Title: A hit must report the page it came from, so R34's official score can be measured
Created: 2026-09-07
Author: claude

--- log ---
2026-09-07T02:34Z claude created

--- body ---
## Context

Ruled 2026-09-07 (`DECISIONS.md`): R34 is satisfied when a reply returns the
right work and a page intersecting the target's page range, and two scores are
tracked, of which only the one reading the system's own reported page is
official.

Measured against the golden run's own artifact before the ruling was written:
of 1 928 hits across 843 replies, **none carries a page**. The reply schema has
the field. The engine returns item key, title, snippet and score, so nothing
fills it.

So the official score is zero today, and stays zero until this gap closes. That
is deliberate — it keeps the gap visible in every run rather than buried in a
ticket — but the gap itself is not a scoring problem. It is R24 unmet:

> **R24. Locator.** A hit MUST lead to the page it came from, and one entry
> MUST give one hit.

A hit that hands back a snippet with no page has not led the reader anywhere.
This ticket is that clause measured, not a new demand.

## What is needed

A retrieval reply must carry, per hit, the page the evidence came from, and say
what kind of page it is: the printed folio where the extraction knows it, an
estimate where it is derived, per D10. Where the passage straddles a boundary
the reply gives the range rather than one number, which is what makes the
ruled intersection test computable on both sides.

The extraction already knows this. Zotero's own full-text cache marks page
breaks with a form feed, which is how the accommodating score derives a page
offline. What is missing is the passage-to-page mapping surviving into the
index and out through the query path.

## Actions

1. Establish where the page is lost: whether the passage record keeps a page
   or an offset at all, and if not, at which stage it is dropped.
2. State what it costs to carry it: the index column, the write path, and
   whether an existing index can gain it without a rebuild (R23, R3).
3. Propose the reply-shape change, and the estimate flag D10 requires.
4. Prepare it as an upstream contribution rather than a fork-local patch: this
   is a promise the shipped product makes, not an artifact of the bench.

## Exit criteria
- [ ] where the page is lost between extraction and reply is established from
      source, not inferred
- [ ] the cost of carrying it stated, including whether a rebuild is implied
- [ ] a reply shape proposed that gives a page or a page range per hit, with
      the estimate flag D10 requires
- [ ] R34's official score becomes measurable, and its first non-zero reading
      is recorded
